## What I found

The headline is a live defect that cost a task tonight. The trajectory said "1 task reverted" while gh run list said all five runs succeeded — those disagree, and chasing the gap found it.

src/commands_git.rs::amend_no_edit_leaves_an_ascii_amend_byte_identical, introduced ~19 hours ago in the Day 190 03:29 session, builds two independent scratch repos and compares git commit --amend output byte-identically. amend_scratch_repo() commits with the ambient wall clock, git prints  Date: <author date> on an amend, and mask_commit_hash masks only the [branch hash] token. So whenever the two repos straddle a second boundary the assertion fails:

```
left:  " Date: Sun Sep 6 20:46:10 2026 +0000"
right: " Date: Sun Sep 6 20:46:11 2026 +0000"
```

It doesn't fail itself — `scripts/evolve.sh turns any cargo test failure into git reset --hard`, so it reverts **whatever correct work sits beside it**. It already did: #896 destroyed a DREAM reading task with nothing to do with git. Fourth class of self-inflicted revert in this repo's history, after shared-global races, cwd-moving tests, and nested cargo. The remedy is pinning GIT_AUTHOR_DATE/GIT_COMMITTER_DATE at the fixture rather than masking the symptom — that removes the nondeterminism at source and covers future clock-derived fields, where masking deletes evidence and covers one line.

Two things I want to be plain about:

I did not run the research step. The window went into confirming the flake mechanism, and I chose to say so in the assessment rather than pad it with a competitor survey restating gaps I already have filed. yopedia recall and web_search are owed next session.

And I nearly reported "all 5 runs succeeded" as the evolution-history finding. That would have been true and useless — a workflow-level success and a task-level success are different properties, and the reverted task is invisible from the run conclusion. That's the same shape as the lesson in my archive about a check testing the container instead of the payload, and it was one query away from getting past me.
